Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.300
The robot dance is the greatest novelty
dance of all time.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:08.440
You can do it if you're a kid,

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:16.340
or an expert. And the robot’s rise is the result of style and technology playing at the same beat.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:20.340
The robot goes back before the word robot was invented.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:26.160
Some of the first films show people doing mannequin dances that have the same stilted motion.

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:32.730
With the invention of the word “robot” in 1921, it was possible to become a new type of creature

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:35.880
for a newly mechanical age.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:42.940
As robots became part of pop culture people sought ways to interpret them. But the modern robot dance has

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:54.200
different parts. In the late 60s, a mime named Robert Shields (and later his partner, Lorenne Yarnell) were inspired by mannequin dances, and Shields brought his

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:59.820
robot onto shows like Merv Griffin’s, where he dissected them with an engineer’s precision.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:06.080
But the dance wouldn’t exist today without the performers who later set it to music.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:12.860
Some were inspired by Shields, and some were independent inventors. Don Campbell —the inventor of Campbellocking—

00:01:12.909 --> 00:01:17.629
locking formed to crew that included
poppers, lockers, and robotters like

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:25.520
Charles “Robot” Washington and Bill “Slim the Robot” Williams (who actually had worked as a living mannequin in a clothing store).

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:28.880
They popped, locked, and robotted in recognizable dances.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:34.539
Soul Train gave them a platform. Key pioneers  like Damita Jo Freeman robotted next to James Brown on TV.

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:37.840
robotted next to James Brown on TV.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:42.900
Together, all these influences — Soul Train, lockers, and the occasional mime — made what we call the robot.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:44.640
lockers and the occasional mind made
what we call the robot but its have

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:50.140
But it tool another spark another spark to make the robot mainstream that spark was the Jackson 5

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:58.600
The Jackson 5 performed their hit 1973 song “Dancing Machine” on Soul Train, but that was just the beginning.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:06.940
There were other songs, like Rufus Thomas’s Funky Robot, but by 1974, Dancing Machine was a worldwide hit.

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:09.320
with world wide professional campaign.

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:12.540
They performed it on the Mike Douglas Show and on Carol Burnett.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:18.540
They telethoned it with Jerry Lewis, showed off for Johnny Carson, and even enlisted Cher to do it with them.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:25.900
The dance, assembled by street dancers, Soul Train, (and a few cutting edge mimes) took over thanks to the Jacksons.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:31.220
They ensured it was forever known as a dancing machine — that is, a robot.

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:36.620
All those parts gave us the robot we all know today, equally enjoyed by uncoordinated five-year olds,

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:40.540
uncles at weddings, and practiced professionals.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:46.600
And it succeeded because for a mechanical dance, the robot is surprisingly flexible.

